Lab 12: Blind SQL Injection with Conditional Errors
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is another form of blind SQL injection, but the signal we rely on is different from the previous lab.
In the last lab, the page showed or hid the text “Welcome back” depending on whether our condition was true.
In this lab, there is no such message at all. Instead, the application shows a generic error whenever the SQL
query itself fails to run. We don’t get to see any data, and the page doesn’t behave differently for true versus
false, except for one thing: if our injected SQL causes a database error, the server responds differently.

Goal: using only the presence or absence of a database error as our signal, extract the administrator’s
password character by character, then log in as administrator.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

Just like the previous lab, the vulnerable input is the TrackingId cookie. Its value is used inside a SQL query
behind the scenes, without being checked or cleaned, so we can inject our own SQL into it.

=============================
STEP 1: PROVE THE INJECTION POINT EXISTS AT ALL

Before trying anything clever, we first confirm that adding a single quote to the cookie breaks the query and
causes a visible error. Then, adding a second quote right after it closes the string back up properly, so the
query becomes valid again and the error disappears. This alone proves that an unclosed quote is enough to cause
a detectable error, which is the foundation the rest of the attack is built on.

=============================
STEP 2: CONFIRM IT IS SPECIFICALLY A SQL ERROR

Just because something errors does not automatically mean it is a SQL syntax error, it could be some unrelated
problem. To rule this out, we attempt to build something that looks like a valid SQL subquery, and see if the
query accepts it without error.

The first attempt tries a generic empty SELECT, joined into the string using double pipes, which is how text is
concatenated together in Oracle databases. If this still errors, it suggests the database might specifically
require every SELECT statement to name a real table, which is a known Oracle requirement. Oracle databases have
a special placeholder table called dual, which exists purely so SELECT statements have something to reference
even when no real table is needed. Once dual is added to the query, the error disappears, which strongly suggests
the backend database is Oracle.

To further confirm the injected text is genuinely being interpreted as SQL, we try referencing a table name that
clearly does not exist. This causes an error again, which makes sense only if the database is actually trying to
look up that table and failing, rather than the error being caused by something unrelated to SQL at all.

=============================
STEP 3: CONFIRM THE USERS TABLE EXISTS

Once we know valid SQL syntax does not error, and that the database is Oracle, we query the known users table,
limiting it to return only one row at a time, since returning more than one row would break the string
concatenation structure we are relying on. If no error appears, this confirms the users table genuinely exists.

=============================
STEP 4: LEARN TO TRIGGER ERRORS CONDITIONALLY

This is the main idea the entire rest of the attack depends on. Using a CASE WHEN structure, we tell the
database: if a certain condition is true, attempt to divide the number 1 by 0, which is mathematically invalid
and always causes an error. If the condition is false, just return an empty piece of text instead, which causes
no error at all.

By testing with a condition we already know is true, like 1 equals 1, we confirm that an error is produced. By
testing with a condition we know is false, like 1 equals 2, we confirm no error appears. This proves we now have
a reliable way to turn any true or false question into either an error or a normal response, entirely under our
control.

=============================
STEP 5: CONFIRM THE ADMINISTRATOR USER EXISTS

Using the same CASE WHEN structure, we now check for a row in the users table where the username equals
administrator. If the condition evaluates as true, the divide by zero runs, and we get an error. Getting that
error confirms the administrator account exists.

=============================
STEP 6: FIND THE PASSWORD LENGTH

Still using the exact same structure, we now test the administrator’s password length, asking repeatedly if it
is greater than 1 character, then 2, then 3, and so on. Each time the condition is true, an error is returned.
The exact point where the error stops happening tells us the password’s real length, which the lab confirms is
20 characters.

=============================
STEP 7: FIND EACH CHARACTER, ONE POSITION AT A TIME

Once the length is known, we use the SUBSTR function to pull out one character at a specific position in the
password, and compare it against a guessed letter or digit. If the guess is correct, the CASE WHEN condition
becomes true, the divide by zero runs, and we get an error. If wrong, no error happens.

We repeat this for every position, trying every lowercase letter and digit until the correct one is found for
that position, then move to the next position, continuing until the full password is revealed.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept the request carrying the TrackingId cookie using Burp Suite.
Add a single quote to the cookie value, and confirm an error appears.
Add a second quote right after it, and confirm the error disappears.
Build a subquery using double pipes and an empty SELECT, testing with and without referencing the dual table,
to confirm the database is Oracle and that it is genuinely interpreting the input as SQL.
Confirm the users table exists by selecting from it with a row limit of one, with no error appearing.
Use a CASE WHEN structure with a divide by zero to confirm that a true condition produces an error, and a false
condition does not.
Confirm the administrator user exists the same way.
Test increasing password lengths until the error stops appearing, revealing the password length.
Use Burp Intruder to automate testing every character at every position, checking which payload causes an HTTP
500 status code instead of 200.
Once every position’s character is found, combine them into the full password, and log in manually as
administrator using that password.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests, and the string library, for easy access to
lowercase letters and digits.

It asks for the base address of the lab, removing any trailing slash.

A session is created, and a request is sent to the home page purely to receive a real TrackingId cookie from the
server, the same way an ordinary visitor would get one. This value is saved, since every injected payload must be
built on top of this real value to keep the query structure valid.

A helper function called send_condition is defined. It takes a SQL condition as plain text, and optionally a
specific FROM clause, since some checks need to reference a real table like users, while others only need the
dual placeholder table. It builds the exact payload structure described in the walkthrough: the original tracking
ID, two pipe symbols, an opening parenthesis, a SELECT CASE WHEN statement using the given condition, a divide by
zero inside the true branch, an empty string in the false branch, the FROM clause, a closing parenthesis, two
more pipe symbols, and a final quote to close the string cleanly. It sends this as the cookie value and returns
True if the response comes back with an HTTP 500 status code, meaning an error occurred, or False if the
response is a normal 200.

The script first tests the condition 1 equals 1, expecting this to cause an error, and separately tests 1 equals
2, expecting no error. Both results are printed clearly, so it is immediately obvious if the basic premise is
working before anything else runs. If this basic check fails, the script stops immediately instead of continuing
with checks that would not make sense otherwise.

It then confirms the administrator user exists, using the same helper function but pointing the FROM clause
directly at the users table filtered by that username.

Next, it searches for the password length, looping through increasing numbers and asking each time if the
length is greater than that number. The loop stops the moment a check comes back false, and that number becomes
the confirmed password length.

After that, it begins the character extraction phase, trying every lowercase letter and digit at each position
in the password, using the SUBSTR function inside the condition. The first character that causes an error at a
given position is recorded as correct, and the script moves on to the next position immediately, rather than
continuing to test further characters that are no longer needed.

Once every position has been resolved, all the found characters are joined together into the final complete
password, which is printed at the end, along with a reminder to log in manually using the administrator username
and this extracted password.

=============================
WHY THE PAYLOAD STRUCTURE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE PREVIOUS LAB

The previous lab used a simple AND condition directly attached to the existing query, since all we needed to do
there was make an existing true or false check affect whether “Welcome back” appeared. This lab has no such
built-in true or false behavior to piggyback on, so we had to build our own condition entirely from scratch using
string concatenation and a subquery, deliberately designed so that a true condition causes a completely
unrelated error, a divide by zero, rather than relying on any existing application behavior.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same as every previous lab, a cookie value is joined directly into a SQL query without
being checked or cleaned. The difference here is purely in how we detect the result, through a deliberately
caused database error instead of a visible page difference or directly displayed data.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that cookie values, like any other user input, can never change the structure or
logic of a SQL query, regardless of what error-triggering logic an attacker might try to insert.

Other good practices:

Avoid showing different error behavior, such as different status codes or error messages, based on the result of
a query built from unsanitized input, since even something as simple as an error appearing or not appearing can
be enough to extract data one bit at a time.
Use generic, non-descriptive error pages for all unexpected server errors, so that no internal detail about the
database or query structure is ever revealed through error responses.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

When there is no visible difference in page content, a deliberately triggered database error, such as a divide
by zero, can be used instead as the true or false signal.
Different databases have different quirks, such as Oracle requiring every SELECT statement to name a real table,
and recognizing these quirks can help confirm exactly which database is being used.
The CASE WHEN structure is a powerful tool for turning any true or false condition into something detectable,
whether that is an error, a delay, or some other observable difference.
This technique still requires confirming the injection point step by step, finding the length first, then testing
every character at every position, exactly like the previous blind injection method, just with a different way of
reading the answer.
